{"id":"qzpyq-physics-dual-nature-of-matter-dual-nature-of-matter-test-6","_quizrrTestId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f","title":"Dual Nature of Matter - Test 6","questions":[{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6db9","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6db9","q":"A particle is travelling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> times as fast as an electron. Assuming the ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of a particle to that of electron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;the mass of the particle is :-","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></math> times the mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math> times the mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>-</mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> times the mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math> times the mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></math><br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></math><br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math><br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dbe","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dbe","q":"The threshold frequency of a metal with work function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>63</mn><mo> </mo><mi>eV</mi></math> is :","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The formula to calculate the work function of the metal is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>From equation (1), it follows that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>63</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>63</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc3","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc3","q":"<p>The work functions of Aluminium and Gold are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math> respectively. The ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for Gold to that of Aluminium is</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>24</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The data given is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#981;</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math></p><p>The energy is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is the stopping potential and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>Thus, the above equation can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>&#981;</mi><mi>e</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>The equation is of the form of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></math>. The slope is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>e</mi></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Since the Planck constant&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math>&nbsp;and charge of an electron is a constant&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi></math>, the slope does not depend on the work function. Hence the ratio of the slope of the plot of stopping potential and frequency for both the given metals is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>.</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc8","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dc8","q":"The kinetic energy of emitted electron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> when the light incident on the metal has wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>. To double the kinetic energy, the incident light must have wavelength:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>Using Einstein&#39;s photoelectric equation, kinetic energy of an electron is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;when the incident wavelength is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>When kinetic energy doubles, then,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Subtracting&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math>, we have&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dcd","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dcd","q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion $\\mathbf{A}$ and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: Number of photons increases with increase in frequency of light.\nReason R: Maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons increases with the frequency of incident radiation.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{R}$ are correct and $\\mathbf{R}$ is the correct explanation of $\\mathbf{A}$.","Both $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{R}$ are correct and $\\mathbf{R}$ is NOT the correct explanation of $\\mathbf{A}$.","$\\mathbf{A}$ is not correct but $\\mathbf{R}$ is correct.","$\\mathbf{A}$ is correct but $\\mathbf{R}$ is not correct."],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Intensity of light $\\mathrm{I}=\\frac{\\mathrm{nh} \\nu}{\\mathrm{A}}$\nHere $\\mathrm{n}$ is no. of photons per unit time.\n$\\mathrm{n}=\\frac{\\mathrm{IA}}{\\mathrm{h} \\nu}$ so on increasing frequency $v, \\mathrm{n}$ decreases taking intensity constant.\n$\\mathrm{k}_{\\max }=\\mathrm{h} v-\\phi$<br  />So on increasing $v$, kinetic energy increases.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd2","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd2","q":"The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelengths of proton and electron having same kinetic energy:<br />(Assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1849</mn></math>)","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>43</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>30</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>62</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>43</mn></math>"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The de-Broglie wavelength is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>(</mo><mtext>As</mtext><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.</p><p>It is given that</p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mi>p</mi></msub></math></p><p>Thus, the ratio of the wavelengths of the proton and electron is&nbsp;<br  />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math><br  />&nbsp; &nbsp;  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msqrt><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>1849</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>43</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd7","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dd7","q":"A monochromatic light is incident on a metallic plate having work function $\\phi$. An electron, emitted normally to the plate from a point $A$ with maximum kinetic energy, enters a constant magnetic field, perpendicular to the initial velocity of electron. The electron passes through a curve and hits back the plate at a point $B$. The distance between A and B is :<br/>(Given : The magnitude of charge of an electron is e and mass is $\\mathrm{m}, \\mathrm{h}$ is Planck's constant and c is velocity of light. Take the magnetic field exists throughout the path of electron)","options":["$\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$","$\\sqrt{\\mathrm{m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$","$\\sqrt{8 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$","$2 \\sqrt{\\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} / \\mathrm{eB}$"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{KE}_{\\max }=\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi \\\\ & \\mathrm{p}=\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{mK}_{\\max }} \\\\ & \\mathrm{p}=\\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)} \\\\ & \\mathrm{d}_{\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{B}}=2 \\mathrm{R} \\\\ & =2\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{p}}{\\mathrm{qB}}\\right]\\end{aligned}$<br/>$\\mathrm{d}_{\\mathrm{AB}}=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)}}{\\mathrm{eB}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{8 \\mathrm{~m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)}}{\\mathrm{eB}}$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6ddc","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6ddc","q":"The de-Broglie wavelength of a particle having kinetic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>.</mo></math> How much extra energy must be given to this particle so that the de-Broglie wavelength reduces to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;of the initial value?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>E</mi><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Recall the formula of de-Broglie wavelength,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></math>, here&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi></math>&nbsp;is the linear momentum of the particle, now use the relation between linear momentum and kinetic energy,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msqrt><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6de1","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6de1","q":"<p>In a photoelectric experiment, ultraviolet light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>280</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math> is used with lithium cathode having work function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>. If the wavelength of incident light is switched to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math>, find out the change in the stopping potential.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>63</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>From the photoelectric equation,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></math></p><p>Using this equation for the given two cases,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>280</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></math></p><p>On subtracting,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#8722;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1240</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mfrac><mn>120</mn><mrow><mn>280</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1.3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#8722;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6de6","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6de6","q":"An <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>&nbsp;particle, a proton and an electron have the same kinetic energy. Which one of the following is correct in case of their de-Broglie wavelength:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>As we know, de-Broglie wavelength is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msqrt></math></p><p>Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math></p><p>As kinetic energy is the same for all particles,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6deb","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6deb","q":"The work functions of cesium (Cs) and lithium (Li) metals are 1.9 eV and 2.5 eV , respectively. If we incident a light of wavelength 550 nm on these two metal surfaces, then photo-electric effect is possible for the case of","options":["Both Cs and Li","Neither Cs nor Li","Cs only","Li only"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"\\(E=\\frac{1240 \\mathrm{eV} \\cdot \\mathrm{~nm}}{550 \\mathrm{~nm}}=2.25 \\mathrm{eV}\\)\r\nPossible for \\(C_s\\) as \\(E  \\gt  \\phi_{c s}\\)\r\nNot for Li as \\(E \\lt  \\phi_{L i}\\)","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6df0","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6df0","q":"The ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-particle and a proton accelerated from rest by the same potential is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math> , the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> is:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>As we know, de Broglie wavelength in terms of energy of particle is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>E</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Energy gained by proton,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>V</mi></math>.</p><p>Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>&#945;</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6df5","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6df5","q":"In photoelectric experiment energy of $2.48 \\mathrm{eV}$ irradiates a photo sensitive material. The stopping potential was measured to be $0.5 \\mathrm{~V}$. Work function of the photo sensitive material is :","options":["$1.68 \\mathrm{eV}$","$2.48 \\mathrm{eV}$","$1.98 \\mathrm{eV}$","$0.5 \\mathrm{eV}$"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{eV} \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{s}}=\\mathrm{h} \\nu-\\phi \\\\ & 0.5 \\mathrm{~V}=2.48-\\phi \\\\ & \\text { work function }(\\phi)=2.48 \\mathrm{~V}-0.5 \\mathrm{~V}=1.98 \\mathrm{~V}\\end{aligned}$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfa","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dfa","q":"An electron moving with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> and a photon moving with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi></math>, have the same D-Broglie wavelength. The ratio of the kinetic energy of the electron to that of a photon is:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></math>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>Ph</mi></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">h</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ph</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">e</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">c</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">v</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">c</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6dff","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6dff","q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : Emission of electrons in photoelectric effect can be suppressed by applying a sufficiently negative electron potential to the photoemissive substance.\nReason (R) : A negative electric potential, which stops the emission of electrons from the surface of a photoemissive substance, varies linearly with frequency of incident radiation.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both \\((\\mathbf{A})\\) and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) are true and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) is the correct explanation of \\((\\mathbf{A})\\)","Both \\((\\mathbf{A})\\) and \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) are true but \\((\\mathbf{R})\\) is not the correct explanation of \\((\\mathbf{A})\\)"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Assertion (A) is true because a sufficiently negative potential can repel and stop the emitted electrons from leaving the surface.\r\nReason (R) is also true because the stopping potential \\(V_{\\text {stop }}\\) varies linearly with the frequency \\(\\nu\\) of the incident light \\(\\left(V_{\\text {stop }}=\\frac{h}{e} \\nu-\\frac{\\phi}{e}\\right)\\).\r\nHowever, (R) does not directly explain why a negative potential suppresses electron emission; it only shows how much potential is needed for different frequencies.\r\nTherefore, both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6e04","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6e04","q":"A moving proton and electron have the same de-Broglie wavelength. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> denote the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>.</mo></math> and momentum respectively. Then choose the correct option :","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math><br  />If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math> is same the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> will be same<br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></math><br  /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6e09","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6e09","q":"From the photoelectric effect experiment, following observations are made. Identify which of these are correct<br />A. The stopping potential depends only on the work function of the metal.<br />B. The saturation current increases as the intensity of incident light increases.<br />C. The maximum kinetic energy of a photo electron depends on the intensity of the incident light.<br />D. Photoelectric effect can be explained using wave theory of light.<br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B, C only","A, C, D only","B only","A, B, D only"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"(A) Stopping potential depends on both frequency of light and work function.<br  />(B) Saturation current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8733;</mo></math> intensity of light.<br  />(C) Maximum KE depends on frequency.<br  />(D) Photoelectric effect is explained using particle theory. In this phenomenon, when electromagnetic radiation (such as light) hits the material, the emission of electrons takes place. In the photoelectric effect, if the frequency is too low, no electron is seen getting freed. But, if the frequency is high enough, some electrons can be observed. These observations prove that the Light is made of particles. The energy of the particle increases with the frequency.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0e","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6e0e","q":"The de Broglie wavelength of a molecule in a gas at room temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. If the temperature of the gas is increased to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>, then the de Broglie wavelength of the same gas molecule becomes","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The root mean squared velocity of a gas is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></math></p><p>Let</p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p>Taking velocity ratios at the given temperatures,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>300</mn><mn>600</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></math></p><p>The de Broglie wavelength is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. So,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>The ratio of the wavelengths is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math></p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6e13","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6e13","q":"When radiation of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math> is incident on a metallic surface, the stopping potential of ejected photoelectrons is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. If the same surface is illuminated by radiation of double the previous wavelength, then the stopping potential becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>. The threshold wavelength of the metal is:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p>Using above equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>iii</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Put in equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>th</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>th</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"},{"id":"qz_69de4efac677dc36a88f6e18","_quizrrId":"69de4efac677dc36a88f6e18","q":"An electron and proton are separated by a large distance. The electron starts approaching the proton with energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>eV</mi></math>. The proton captures the electrons and forms a hydrogen atom in second excited state. The resulting photon is incident on a photosensitive metal of threshold wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4000</mn><mi>A</mi></math> What is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectron?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>61</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>41</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>","No photoelectron would be emitted"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Dual Nature of Matter","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Initially, energy of electron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p><p>finally, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">2</mi><mrow><mi>nd</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msup></math> excited state,</p><p>energy of electron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>eV</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p><p>Loss in energy is emitted as photon,</p><p>So, photon energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p><p>Now, photoelectric effect equation</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KE</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mi>hc</mi><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>th</mi></msub></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn><mi>eV</mi><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12400</mn><mi>eVA</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4000</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>41</mn><mi>eV</mi></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b0f"}]}